let's broaden out a little bit and  discuss where we go from here  i'm joined by three women one of  westminster's most independent  minded mps diane abbott also  the co-founder of the women's equality  party catherine mayer  and as well the co-chair of the mayor of  london's violence  against women and girls panel joan smith  thank you all so much for joining us  this morning um  ask you all the same question first and  then we'll move on um cressida dick is  it time for her to go diane abbott  we would hope that if she goes  that she's actually replaced with  somebody better  someone that doesn't deny institutional  racism exists in the police force  and someone with a much less  heavy-handed  approach to public order issues  catherine  uh i think that her position is  untenable but the danger  of just seeing her resign is that this  will then become  a problem about her leadership as  opposed to being something systemic  as opposed to being something that is  not just a failure  of policing and the police culture but  also of politics  and i'm really quite worried that rather  like the way the metoo movement sort of  unleashed all of these testimonies  but then kind of drew back from  the kind of systemic change we needed  that we're at exactly a kind of  moment where the same thing could happen  we we can make in  we can make progress now people see what  the problem is  but they will be very easily distracted  and in a horrible way  this um churn with  cressida at the top of the police  this discussion the discussion of what  happened last night  it's all very important but it mustn't  distract from the bigger issue okay  and joan very briefly uh do you think we  should  be asking if she should go do you think  she should go  my personal view is that she's  mishandled this right from the start  right going back to wednesday when i  i was really taken aback by the  statement she put out when the  when the metropolitan police announced  they'd found human remains and i think  she's got the tone wrong from the start  and i agree that she knows her going  is actually not the most important thing  at the moment but i do think there's a  serious question about leadership at the  metropolitan police  okay dan let's go back to you um  a lot of women have been sharing their  experiences this week of harassment on  the street  of sexism in the workplace you shared  your experiences  you've talked about uh hearing a man's  footsteps and crossing the road as a  habit of a lifetime  have things got better do you think from  from those days when you first got  elected into parliament back in 1987  um just on the cretader dick question i  think it's important to remember that  the local  police in lamberth wanted the peaceful  vigil to go ahead or willing to work  with you  it was crested a dick and people at  scotland yard  that insisted on banning it all together  when everybody knew people would turn up  anyway  so she does have questions to answer in  relation  to experience of harassment on the  street i think in some ways it's got  worse  because what you've got is the online  world  which feeds um  hostility and violence against women  um and i was struck when i started to  think about it  how much i had always taken it for  granted  that the potential for violence by men  on the street was just something that  you  you had to guard against by crossing the  road  catherine do you think there is a link  through all of this  with with what you were trying to do  with women's equality party  fighting for equality equal pay equal  representation  this low level of misogyny do you think  that that is the root of the problem  that that then feeds into  the broader picture absolutely i mean  one of the things we've been seeing in  the pandemic  um the ways in which inequalities of all  kinds are laid bare the people  who are already vulnerable have been  made more vulnerable it has  been gendered in its impacts it has  hit people of color harder it has hit  people with disabilities in different  and harder ways and what you see  play out now with with this  and with this whole discussion in fact  is  um as as what we're all trying to talk  about  is the endemic nature of this  and the fact that it has not been  tackled it is never tackled it always  um you know you'll see tinkering about  the edges  but it isn't treated like terrorism  or as a form of terrorism as something  that is absolutely  central and that is linked to all these  different sort of  cultural strains to an education system  two forms of inequality  that feed back into this and amplify it  and so  what you need to do is in a moment like  this where you can see those mechanisms  you need to lay them there and you need  to look for  leadership but you know in the political  sphere  i keep saying that there are these  excellent women like diane who have been  working for years trying to push this up  the agenda and yet it doesn't happen  and yet here we are at our age seeing it  in many ways as diane said getting worse  not better  so this is a question about priority  this is a question about understanding  the ways in which all of those different  inequalities make this  not just tragic but tragically  inevitable  and you know this is this is preventable  but it is only preventable  if it is made a national priority if  um men step up to the plate if male  politicians recognize this and if there  is a  a wider um a conversation  about and not just a conversation i mean  we have a a whole load of very specific  policy proposals on this um  including indeed making it  a national priority um  on on the you know um it should be  identified as a national threat  violence against women okay jess spring  and joan i mean i mean joan you  you know you've talked about living in a  society where some  humans are worth less than others do you  think  and as katherine alluded to that  possibly we have hit a turning point  possibly  something very good could come of this  very very difficult week  well i very much like to think so but at  the same time i feel a kind of  disconnect between what people  say on occasions like this when they're  horrified by  what's happened to a particular woman  which is absolutely horrendous and i  think we you know we all feel very  distressed about it  but some of these people who are  expressing you know  shock and outrage now are the same  people who will carelessly read about a  rape trial  in the newspaper and say god you know  why was she on her own at night why did  she go to that man's hotel room  so i think there's a kind of you know  this is there's always these moments  where it feels as if things are going to  change but what we've actually got  is a broken justice system and that  reflects  the misogyny of the culture that we  inhabit  so for years now we've been having we've  had so many rape reviews i could  probably paper the walls of my house  with their findings  it doesn't change anything the you know  the conviction rate keeps going down and  that is because  of suspicion and disbelief towards women  that it's very easy to say gosh what  happened to this woman was particularly  awful  but actually what's happening to  thousands millions of women is awful  but we don't believe them when they tell  their stories and politicians  actually you know there may be a bit of  you know at the moment politicians  saying yes this is actually this is  dreadful  but where are the national campaigns  where are politics where are  candidates mps councillors getting up  and saying  this is this is terrible this is enough  this has got to stop we have to change  the culture  right from the start i mean that's a  that's a good point isn't it diana  average i mean  we look at the government anyway  you know we're half of the country but  we're only a fifth of those who are in  the cabinet  only 34 to the house of commons 24 to  the conservative party um  westminster's been labeled pest minster  in the past because of issues  uh there with sexual harassment i mean  are we wrong to look to politicians to  change this you know should we be  looking  somewhere else oh in the end  when you want to see substantive change  in a society  the political process is part of that  and to be fair some of us as women mps  have spoken out about these things  but in the past it hasn't necessarily  been seen as news by the media  we need a change in culture we need the  media to take these things seriously not  when there is one dramatic case  but there's the steady reality  of violence against women but yes at the  end of the day  this needs legislation this needs  funding and this needs political action  and catherine briefly a lot of men this  week have been questioning what they  can do to make women feel safer you know  i was chatting to my husband about it  and he said well i always cross the road  if i'm  on my own on the street and there's a  woman because it's just something i was  taught when i was younger but  a lot of men don't do that do you think  that  that  possibly we will see a change in society  from men  understanding now what women silently go  through in their head  well diane's point about the media is  very important here it's great that  you're doing a whole program on it but  it needs to be the whole media  talking about this all the time and in  ways that  really make people understand and make  men understand that this is  their issue so it isn't just about  making women feel safer on the street in  those small ways  it's about understanding this as a daily  experience  for women that disfigures the whole of  society  you know that whole kind of thing about  not women trending again  and you know the point is it may not be  all men  who commit violence but it is all women  and it is  all men for whom this is an issue too i  mean one of the most bizarre things is  seeing people seeking  to minimize the experience of women by  saying that more men are  murdered than women yes by men 96  of murderers are men it is a problem for  men and it needs to be seen as one  that is for men to step up to the plate  and solve  so the awareness that you're talking  about is really important but it goes  way beyond this  this is a time for men to actually  understand  that it is in everybody's interest for  them to tackle the problem of male  violence  and joan just last thought from you you  referred to all the different reports  that have been carried out  uh into violence and how you could have  wallpapered your home with them  suzanne moore funnily enough this week  said that misogyny is the wallpaper  that we live with do you have any real  hope that that society  will change only if we can keep this  going and if men ask themselves  questions like  it's not just what do i do if i'm  walking down a dark street and i don't  want to frighten a woman  it's also what happens when your friend  makes a really nasty rape joke  or says something demeaning about women  that nobody else overhears  do you act on that um i think you know  men have to look at their own private  behavior as well and ask themselves  you know are they tolerating a degree of  misogyny which i'm afraid i've been  writing about them for most of my adult  life and you know it's disappointing  that  that we are still here but i do think  it's up to men to look at their own  attitudes the attitudes of their friends  and family  as well as you know talking about what  happens in public  joan smith catherine mayer and diana but  thank you all very much  for joining us for this special program

